{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/58191-divine-hotch-potch","id":58191,"title":"Divine Hotch Potch","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-01-26T11:08:31-08:00","id":58191,"title":"Divine Hotch Potch","content":"Alright, let me lay out a scenario for you. You're the ST for your group's Scion game and your Players ask you what Pantheons there allowed to play. You being the nice guy ST decides to take a liberal approach to the question and say, &quot;pick any you want.&quot; The end result is that you now have a band of Scions that come from widely different cultures and possess Virtues that seem to constantly conflict with each other. For example, a Dodekatheon Scion with a lot Vengeance thinks that those cops who had been shooting at the Band two scenes deserve to have their family homes burned to the fucking ground, while the Deva Scion with Order understands that this act of revenge motivated arson would be quite obviously against the local laws. As you'd probably think figure out this kind of Virtue conflict leads to a whole lot of in-character fights that take up way to much time and could possibly lead to a disastrous instance of PC vs PC brawls. And don't even get me started about how most PC's of different cultures are going to react to the Teotl's need for human sacrifices, the Kami's overly formal rules of etiquette or the Dodekatheon's ok-ness with male-on-male homosexuality. <br /> <br /> So, to make a long point short, I wanted to hear everyone's opinions on whether to allow Players to freely pick and choose their Pantheons regardless of problems with cultural differences or Virtue incompatibility, or the alternate option to limit them to Pantheons that either share a couple Virtues or have a somewhat similar culture, like the Tuatha and Aesir or the Kami and the Celestial Bureaucracy. In my mind just saying that there is a Titan War going on and that all the Pantheons have somehow got over the deeply entrenched xenophobia to fight the good fight together just doesn't seem to hold water to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1675-weirdboyz","name":"Weirdboyz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"212"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-26T11:50:41-08:00","id":58221,"title":"","content":"The hodgepodge approach works if there's something in the &quot;story&quot; that unifies the group. There should be some element that got the group together, and kept it together.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-26T16:51:33-08:00","id":58400,"title":"","content":"I have not had too many problems with mixed pantheons. The scions of Horus and Tlaloc often butt heads over sacrifices, but nobody has yet to use their virtues as an excuse to be an {fill in favorite expletive}. I do agree with Shadowflame, though, as it is easier if the scions have a reason to be together. In my game, the PCs' pantheons are all that remain and they are forced to work together, whatever their differences.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1011-phgraves","name":"PHGraves","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1011_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-27T03:46:58-08:00","id":58613,"title":"","content":"TBH, I do not see the issue. I have two PC's who are at each other's throats. The Yazata is constantly doing subtle mind-screws to the Amatsukami. They have not -yet- come to actual combat but when they do, then things will be interesting. But, as has been said before, the purpose of the chronicle should come first and help force them to work together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-27T12:20:48-08:00","id":58994,"title":"","content":"What kind of story do you want to do? Is it primarily about the mission itself or more about the people doing the mission? For the former situation the \u201cwell-oiled team\u201d works as long as the scenarios have enough puzzle and mystery factor to carry them and is generally similar to the old-style \u201cblack and white\u201d story with clearly defined \u201cgood guys\u201d and \u201cbad guys\u201d or at least \u201cUs\u201d and \u201cThem\u201d.<br /> <br /> With so much focused on the intricacy of the plot itself the characters in those stories tend to be simpler and somewhat formula but that simplicity can be a virtue in itself when painting a picture of epic good versus evil with broad pure strokes. It is a legitimate style and many of the old classics are set up like that though it has fallen somewhat out of favor nowadays.<br /> <br /> In gaming, a campaign using this style is especially good as a \u201cdetox\u201d for a troupe coming down off of a campaign featuring intense character study in morally gray intrigue and backbiting competition such as Vampire stories. Possible caveats are coming up with a steady supply of interesting plot puzzles and finding interesting ways to bring in the characters into each story in this often episodic style while avoiding falling into a predictable \u201cfreak of the week\u201d format. On the other hand, with its tendency towards stasis it is easier to mix and match episodic scenarios and keep the campaign going than it is to do the same with more serial character-driven campaigns.<br /> <br /> In a campaign of this type you might indeed need to limit the characters to compatible Pantheons or even require them to all be from the same one though on the other hand it is also easy to simply gloss over the differences since the plot and not the characters are the focus.<br /> <br /> The currently in favor \u201cmaverick hero\u201d format looks more at the early stages of team formation where the characters are still working out their differences and usually focuses more on the characters and character development than the plot. It too is a classic theme that many books and movies use in some way, from &quot;coming of age&quot; movies like &quot;The Breakfast Club&quot; to action movies like &quot;The League of Extraordinary Gentlemen&quot; or the &quot;Avengers&quot; movie and a more extreme version in the TV series &quot;Once Upon a Time&quot; where even the main villains of the first season become part of the hero group by the third. Eventually the heroes have to move on to the later stages of team operation or it gets an unrealistic forced feel.<br /> <br /> The strength of this type is that people are usually more able to relate to people rather than things like plot and the interest is usually higher because of it. It is also usually a more complex mix with a wider story range, it being a mesh of each individual characters stories woven together with the background and current plots. It is easier to come up with plots for it since they are not the main focus, anything that gets the characters interacting and pointed in the same general direction is usually sufficient and the plots start writing themselves after a while. Even the trouble of getting the characters inserted into each story\u2019s plot in turn is avoided since most plots come from the characters themselves instead of being discrete outside constructs. Characters for this format should probably not be restricted since the further apart they are at the start the longer the settling in process takes and that is where much of the interest is.<br /> <br /> That strength, like the Force, has a dark side too in that it is easier for the overall story arc to bog under the complexity and/or lose focus with each character going down their own rabbit hole. Also, the need for offbeat characters can lead to extremes in the pursuit of uniqueness such as happened in the entertainment industry where the latest fashion is to try protagonists who are high-functioning autistic / hyper realists like in \u201cBones\u201d, OCD characters like \u201cMonk\u201d, a hallucinating schizophrenic in \u201cPerception\u201d, and even an all-out psychopath in \u201cDexter\u201d.<br /> <br /> Change is a very big part of the style, the \u201clone heroes\u201d of the beginning of the story who cooperate about as much as the Three Stooges going up a ladder and often trip each other up grandstanding must eventually give way to the uneasy alliance stage and finally to the \u201cfamily\u201d stage where the individual characters may not agree with each other\u2019s methods and perhaps even dislike each other to some degree but have enough ties with each other that they present a united front to outsiders. This is the main focus of the genre and the plot is simply the vehicle by which it is done.<br /> <br /> The \u201cPaladin Problem\u201d where a character is absolutely rigidly fixed in their ways can be a major stumbling block in this format, there must be some give somewhere so the group can eventually mesh even if it takes a while. Characters have to find ways to be true to the spirit of their beliefs while adjusting the details to something that is at least tolerable by the others. In the case of the sacrifices, perhaps instead of a ritual with some innocent or whatever on the chopping block the character can arrange it so that the enemies slain in battle suffice instead for instance though most of the problems that need solving would probably not be as big.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":869,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/869-bc001","user":"BC001","date":". 01-27-2014, 03:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/869-bc001","name":"BC001","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar869_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"96"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}